From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000014 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sf2000-linux <sf2000-linux@example.invalid>
Date: Mon, 11 May 2026 10:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mips: sf2000: adopt locked ROM EBase

Real SF2000 hardware reports the ROM handoff EBase as 0x81002000 and ignores
the loader's attempt to move it back to the normal 0x80000000 vector base.
Linux was still installing handlers at the assumed base, so any exception
during early TLB setup would branch to the ROM-selected page instead of the
kernel vectors.

After attempting to normalize CP0 EBase, read it back and adopt the hardware
value if it did not move. The DT already reserves the bootloader scratch area
around physical 0x01000000, so installing exception vectors at 0x81002000 is
preferable to copying vectors into low memory that the CPU is not using.
---
 arch/mips/kernel/traps.c | 18 +++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/mips/kernel/traps.c b/arch/mips/kernel/traps.c
index 742a45a85ac1..d999881bf8f0 100644
--- a/arch/mips/kernel/traps.c
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/traps.c
@@ -130,15 +130,27 @@
 static void __init sf2000_normalize_cp0_ebase(void)
 {
 	unsigned int before;
+	unsigned int requested;
 	unsigned int after;
+	unsigned int actual;
 
 	if (!sf2000_rom_handoff_present())
 		return;
 
+	requested = ebase & MIPS_EBASE_BASE;
 	before = read_c0_ebase();
-	write_c0_ebase(ebase & MIPS_EBASE_BASE);
+	write_c0_ebase(requested);
 	back_to_back_c0_hazard();
 	after = read_c0_ebase();
+	actual = after & MIPS_EBASE_BASE;
 
-	pr_info("sf2000: CP0 EBase %08x -> %08x\n", before, after);
+	if (actual != requested) {
+		ebase = actual;
+		pr_info("sf2000: CP0 EBase %08x ignored request %08x, using %08x\n",
+			before, requested, after);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	ebase = requested;
+	pr_info("sf2000: CP0 EBase %08x -> %08x\n", before, after);
 }
-- 
